Subject: bug in hypergeometric rng ?
Message-ID: <YjHWVD9yIbE.eJHLuWkHQAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/YjHWVD9yIbE/m/eJHLuWkHQAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi

I think there is a bug with hypergeometric_rng function (or at least with its documentation)

I have this stan file:
data {}
parameters { }
model {}

generated quantities{
  real n;
  n<-hypergeometric_rng(10,80,20);
}

When I run it from R I see this:
> sm <- stan_model(file="ttt.stan")
> res <- sampling(sm,data=data,iter=1, chains=1, algorithm="Fixed_param")

SAMPLING FOR MODEL 'ttt' NOW (CHAIN 1).
[1] "Error : Exception thrown at line 7: Error in function boost::math::cdf(const hypergeometric_distribution<double>&, const double&): Random variable out of range: must be less than both n and r but got 40"
error occurred during calling the sampler; sampling not done

Stan documentation (2.9.0) says:

> int hypergeometric_rng(int N, real a, real b)
> Generate a hypergeometric variate with N trials, total success count of a, and
> total failure count of b; may only be used in generated quantities block

Seems to me that so long as N<=a+b there shouldn't be a problem. So what's wrong?

Another issue:
If I change the sampling line to:
 n<-hypergeometric_rng(10,80.5,20.5);

I get a compiler error:

SYNTAX ERROR, MESSAGE(S) FROM PARSER:

No matches for:

  hypergeometric_rng(int, real, real)

Available argument signatures for hypergeometric_rng:

  hypergeometric_rng(int, int, int)

so obviously, the documentation is wrong regarding input types.

yet another issue: in general, to use this function I need to covert  real values (parameters) to ints. Why is there not real to int conversion?

If the problem is simply wrong documentation, I'd like to know the correct usage so that I can work around this.

Thanks !

