From uf13fe7e880ed6103 at groups.google.com  Tue Jun  7 21:21:00 2016
From: uf13fe7e880ed6103 at groups.google.com (Seth Flaxman)
Date: Tue, 07 Jun 2016 21:21:00 +0000
Subject: weird behavior of exponential_log in model vs generated quantities block; something to do with constraints?
Message-ID: <wr2MDcbPWm4.MZ1d70kdAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/wr2MDcbPWm4/m/MZ1d70kdAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi. I can't figure out what is going on with this very simple example
(Stan inline w/ R code, apologies in advance):

fit = stan(model_code='
parameters {
  real<lower=0> y;
}
model {
  increment_log_prob(exponential_log(y,1)); // equivalently: y ~ exponential(1);
}
generated quantities {
  real lp;
  lp <- exponential_log(y,1);
}')

out=extract(fit)
plot(out$lp__,out$lp)

I'm expecting lp and lp__ to be the same, but they're not. lp gives
exactly what I expect, same as dexp(...) in R (since the rate is 1,
the log-likelihood is -y). Can anyone explain what's going on with
lp__?

For comparison, this works as expected:

fit = stan(model_code='
parameters {
  real y;
}
model {
  increment_log_prob(normal_log(y,1,1));
}
generated quantities {
  real lp;
  lp <- normal_log(y,1,1);
}')
out=extract(fit)
plot(out$lp__,out$lp)

And most puzzingly, this does what I expect (although a bunch of
exceptions are thrown whenever y is negative):

fit = stan(model_code='
parameters {
  real y;
}
model {
  increment_log_prob(exponential_log(y,1));
}
generated quantities {
  real lp;
  lp <- exponential_log(y,1);
}')
out=extract(fit)
plot(out$lp__,out$lp)

So I guess the behavior in the first model has something to do with
the constraints on y...but that doesn't make sense to me.

Thanks,
Seth

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue Jun  7 21:33:38 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 07 Jun 2016 21:33:38 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] weird behavior of exponential_log in model vs generated quantities block; something to do with constraints?
Message-ID: <wr2MDcbPWm4.HNcMb_odAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <wr2MDcbPWm4.MZ1d70kdAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <wr2MDcbPWm4.MZ1d70kdAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/wr2MDcbPWm4/m/HNcMb_odAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes, you're getting the Jacobian term from the
inverse of the log-transfrom of y to the unconstrained
space.  Specifically, that's an extra log(y) term,
because y is transformed to log(y) and the inverse transform
is exp, and the log derivate of that evaluated at log(y)
is just log(y).

- Bob

