In the manual, see the chapter on "Transformations of Constrained Variables" for gory details.

To fix your example:
fit = stan(model_code='
parameters {
  real<lower = 0> y;

}
model {
  increment_log_prob(normal_log(y,1,1));
}
generated quantities {
  real lp;
  lp <- normal_log(y,1,1);

  lp <- lp + log(y);
}')
out=extract(fit)
plot(out$lp__,out$lp)

On Tue, Jun 7, 2016 at 5:33 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> Yes, you're getting the Jacobian term from the
> inverse of the log-transfrom of y to the unconstrained
> space.  Specifically, that's an extra log(y) term,
> because y is transformed to log(y) and the inverse transform
> is exp, and the log derivate of that evaluated at log(y)
> is just log(y).
>
> - Bob
>
>
> > On Jun 7, 2016, at 5:20 PM, Seth Flaxman <fla...@gmail.com> wrote:
