MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There's no Metropolis step in NUTS.  See the Hoffman
and Gelman JMLR paper.

In basic HMC, if you simulate the Hamiltonian accurately, the
acceptance probability is 100%, but that's not usually
a good tradeoff for speed (because you need a low step
size).

In either algorithm, you can get repeated draws.

Just dumping out all the draws is more conventional in
my experience, as it feeds more neatly into things like
the autocorrelation algorithm we use for computing effective
sample size.  We could do counts when there are repeats, but
then we'd have to change all of our downstream algorithms.

- Bob

> On Jun 22, 2016, at 5:29 PM, Gregory Martinez <gregory.dav...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Quick question ...
>

> In the HMC STAN uses, there is a Metropolis-Hastings acceptance criteria applied to new proposal points.  Normally, if rejected, the current point is repeated in the M-H acceptance algorithm.  Usually, this is specified my assigned a multiplicity to the points -- e.g. the number of times a point is repeated.  In the STAN output (via the "extract()" member method in pystan) there seems to be neither multiplicity values nor are points repeated in the chain.  Could you please explain? -- Thank you.
