I am attempting to build a model describing economic choices.  I would like to model noise in subjects' estimates of probability and value.  It is known that people exhibit scalar variability, i.e. the standard deviation in their estimate of magnitude grows linearly with the magnitude.  So, I would like to model the subjects' perception of reward amount/probability, accounting for the linear noise scaling.  Something like this:
perceived_rprob[trial_i] <- normal(rightprob[trial_i], rightprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);

However, I get the following error: no matches for normal(real, real).  Function normal not found.  So I must be miscasting the variables? I have also tried the line below instead, but from what I can tell from this group and the documentation, the ~ increments the log posterior, and does not mean "drawn from."  This throws an error that the dimension of the right side of the statement is 0.

perceived_rprob[trial_i] ~ normal(rightprob[trial_i], rightprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);

I am using rstan; my code is below.  I apologize if I am doing something super dumb, any help is very much appreciated.  Thank you so much!!!

model_code <- "data {

    int nTrials;

    int choices[nTrials];  //values of 1 (chose left) or 2 (chose right)

    real<lower=0, upper=1> leftprob[nTrials];

    real<lower=0, upper=1> leftvol[nTrials];  //rewards are normalized by max reward

    real<lower=0, upper=1> rightprob[nTrials];

    real<lower=0, upper=1> rightvol[nTrials];  //rewards are normalized by max reward

}

transformed data {

// this executes once vs on each sample in the model block, so better to transform here and pay for the conditional once

int<lower=0,upper=1> choices_01[nTrials];

for (i in 1:nTrials){

choices_01[i] <- (choices[i]==2);

}

}

parameters {

    real rho; //exponential of utility function

    real alpha;  //parameter for probability weighting function

    real<lower=0> lambda; //softmax parameter

    real<lower=0> prob_m; //slope of scalar variability relationship

    real<lower=0> prob_offset; //offset of scalar variability relationship

    real<lower=0> vol_m;

    real<lower=0> vol_offset;

}

model {

    real Rval[nTrials];

    real Lval[nTrials];

    real perceived_rprob[nTrials];

    real perceived_lprob[nTrials];

    real perceived_rvol[nTrials];

    real perceived_lvol[nTrials];

    rho ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);

    for (trial_i in 1:nTrials) {

     perceived_rprob[trial_i] <- normal(rightprob[trial_i], rightprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);

     perceived_lprob[trial_i] <- normal(leftprob[trial_i], leftprob[trial_i]*prob_m+prob_offset);

     perceived_rvol[trial_i] <- normal(rightvol[trial_i], rightvol[trial_i]*vol_m+vol_offset);

     perceived_lvol[trial_i] <- normal(leftvol[trial_i], leftvol[trial_i]*vol_m+vol_offset);

    Rval <- exp(-(-log(perceived_rprob[trial_i]))^alpha)*(perceived_rvol[trial_i]^rho);

    Lval <- exp(-(-log(perceived_lprob[trial_i]))^alpha)*(perceived_lvol[trial_i]^rho);

    choices_01[trial_i] ~ bernoulli_logit(lambda*(Rval-Lval));

    }

}"

