From u7794973310e701f9 at groups.google.com  Wed Jun 29 19:55:45 2016
From: u7794973310e701f9 at groups.google.com (Yaohui Zeng)
Date: Wed, 29 Jun 2016 19:55:45 +0000
Subject: Why are there exceptions related to NaN values during sampling?
Message-ID: <Utko3usdzCg.nD9Pg2fZCAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/Utko3usdzCg/m/nD9Pg2fZCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear RStan users,

I am implementing an Emax model to model the logit of probability of success for binary-outcome data. During sampling, the following exception was thrown out frequently.
> Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:
> Exception thrown at line 244: stan::math::binomial_logit_log: Probability parameter[1] is -nan, but must be finite!
> If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,
> but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.

The related code snippet is as follows (the actual model/code is really messy so I just pick out the related portion).

data {
...
}

parameters {
  vector[2] logphiA;
  real<lower=0> sigmaA;

  // npara_DLT = 4: alpha_beta_DLT = (e0, emax, log_hill, log_ec50);
  vector[npara_DLT] alpha_beta_DLT;

  ...
}

model {
  logphiA ~ multi_normal(mulogphiA, Sigma_logphiA);
  sigmaA ~ lognormal(mulogsigmaA, sigma_logsigmaA);
  alpha_beta_DLT ~ multi_normal(muDLT, SigmaDLT);

 // data likelihood: logX and Y: 9-by-1 vector;
  logX ~ normal(logphiA[1] + exp(logphiA[2]) * rescale_log_dA, sqrt_vec(pow(sigmaA, 2) ./ NPK));
  Y ~ gamma((NPK - 1) / 2.0, 1 / (2 * pow(sigmaA, 2)));

  vector[NdA] logit_pi_adm_A;
  vector[NdA] logit_pi_adm_A_corrected;

  // Emax model: logit_prob = e0 + emax * (exp(log_hill * (logX - log_EC50) / (1 + exp(log_hill * (logX - log_EC50)))))
  logit_pi_adm_A <- alpha_beta_DLT[1] + alpha_beta_DLT[2] * (exp(alpha_beta_DLT[3] * (logX- alpha_beta_DLT[4])) ./ (1 + exp(alpha_beta_DLT[3] * (logX - alpha_beta_DLT[4]))));

  // print(logit_pi_adm_A);

  // the following is just to restrict logit_prob within (-20, 20)
  logit_pi_adm_A_corrected <- logit_pi_adm_A + step_vec(-logit_crit - logit_pi_adm_A) .* (-logit_crit - logit_pi_adm_A) + step_vec(logit_pi_adm_A - logit_crit) .* (logit_crit - logit_pi_adm_A);
  DLT ~ binomial_logit(NDLT, logit_pi_adm_A_corrected); // This is Line 244

  ...
}

What strange to me is that, if I print out "logit_pi_adm_A" for debugging, there were no NaN values at all (at least for 200 iterations). So I am not sure why there are such exceptions during sampling. I assume this issue affects the convergence:

Warning messages:
1: There were 3928 divergent transitions after warmup. Increasing adapt_delta above 0.8 may help.
2: There were 10009 transitions after warmup that exceeded the maximum treedepth. Increase max_treedepth above 10.
3: Examine the pairs() plot to diagnose sampling problems

I'd really appreciate any comments. Thank you very much!

Yaohui

